[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 359,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 159,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55856,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 159,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory fibroid polyp is a benign, often polypoid, hypocellular fibroblastic neoplasm with a predilection for the stomach and ileum, containing a prominent inflammatory infiltrate, especially eosinophils.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 360,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 160,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55857,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 160,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 361,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 161,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55858,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 161,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA98.2 Inflammatory fibroid polyp of small intestine</p>\r\n<p>DB35.4 Inflammatory fibroid polyp of large intestine</p>\r\n<p>XA6452 Small intestine</p>\r\n<p>XA9780 Duodenum</p>\r\n<p>XA8UM1 Jejunum</p>\r\n<p>XA0QT6 Ileum</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 362,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 162,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55859,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 162,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 363,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 163,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55860,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55861,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The stomach (especially the antrum) is the most commonly affected site, followed by the ileum, although inflammatory fibroid polyps may arise throughout the GI tract {721077; 8214261}. Tumours are usually centred in the submucosa but can be restricted to the mucosa.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55862,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Small tumours in the upper GI tract can be discovered incidentally at endoscopy. Larger tumours may present with abdominal pain, bleeding, or obstruction; intussusception is a common presentation for small intestine tumours. Tumours may be sessile or polypoid in appearance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55863,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory fibroid polyps are rare. There is a slight female predominance and a peak incidence in middle-aged adults {20393746}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55864,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours are sporadic, but rare cases are familial {6745724}, associated with germline <em>PDGFRA</em> mutations {25975287}. Mutation carriers often develop multiple tumours, including gastrointestinal stromal tumours.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55865,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>PDGFRA</em> activating mutations are identified in many cases, most often in small intestine tumours {18686281; 19448595; 20393746}; exon&nbsp;18 mutations, usually c.2525A&gt;T (p.D842V), predominate in gastric tumours, whereas exon&nbsp;12 mutations are nearly always identified in small intestine tumours {22394371}. Germline <em>PDGFRA</em> mutations have been found in the rare familial cases {25975287}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55866,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory fibroid polyps range widely in size; gastric tumours are usually &lt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;cm, whereas ileal tumours are often larger. The cut surface is white and often fleshy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55867,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histological features include a poorly marginated, hypocellular lesion containing a haphazard arrangement of short spindled and stellate cells with fine chromatin, small or indistinct nucleoli, and scant eosinophilic cytoplasm. The stroma is often oedematous and sometimes myxoid or collagenous, with a prominent mixed inflammatory infiltrate, often rich in eosinophils and lymphocytes. Small and intermediate-sized, rounded blood vessels, some with concentric (onion-skin) fibrosis, are a typical finding.</p>\r\n<p>By immunohistochemistry, CD34 and PDGFRA are positive in most cases {9160309; 19448595}. More-limited SMA expression is observed in a small subset of tumours. KIT, DOG1 (ANO1), desmin, S100, SOX10, and keratins are negative.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55868,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55869,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 171,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 374,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55870,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 174,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a hypocellular appearance, with short spindled to stellate cells in an oedematous or myxoid stroma; an inflammatory infiltrate of eosinophils and lymphocytes.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> Expression of CD34.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 372,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55871,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 172,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 373,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55872,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 194,
        "headingId": 173,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory fibroid polyps are benign.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]